From c3c0b12727526b0510c4d5f040870eacfafbf3de Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Liszt <liszt@localhost>
Date: Tue, 11 Aug 2026 13:47:22 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/3] intake: give the layer choice a decision procedure and
 check it against the chain

The layer field kept coming back wrong in the same way: a chain that plainly
runs on hosts, labelled L3 because an agent was the actor. The prompt said to
choose the layer the attack operates at, but gave no procedure for choosing and
no way to be wrong out loud.

Both intake prompts now carry an ordered test for the layer, the anti-patterns
we have actually seen, a closed seam-tag vocabulary for the per-step field, and
a definition of what the source tiers grade. The JSON skeleton no longer shows a
real layer value, because a model handed an example will sometimes return it.
Each prompt ends by asking for a short justification, a runner-up and a
confidence, which the importer puts in front of the reviewer.

On import, the chosen layer is now cross-checked against the seam tags in the
chain. When no step lands on the claimed layer, the record is flagged rather
than corrected: both directions are legitimate answers and only a person can
say which. Source tiers are coerced from words and placeholders, and a step tag
that is really a stack layer is named as such.
